course: everyday
lesson: 472
title: Rival teams and the older bully
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every May, Mill Lane Juniors play Station Road Rovers, and the Rovers' parents chant «Mill Lane, Mill Lane, can't play a game!» Mill Lane lose 4–1 and Sam misses a sitter (an easy chance to score). The captains still shake hands, as the rules say. Next year Mill Lane win 2–1 and Sam scores. A rival is someone you compete against, not someone you hate. Sam's swimming rival Dev beats him by half a second every gala, so Sam trains at 6am three times a week; at the county gala he wins by a fingertip, and Dev says, «About time, mate.»
explain: A bully is different. Big Gaz from Year 8 grabs Sam's new ball at the rec (the recreation ground, a public park) and boots it into the river: «Go fetch, little man.» It floats towards the weir, a low wall across the river where water pours over and pulls hard. Sam does not go in. He goes home and tells his dad, who sees Gaz's mum, and Gaz has to buy a new ball, sulking.
explain: Older bullies count on silence. A Year 6 boy tells Kieran, «Give us your dinner money or else», and Kieran pays, day after day, until on day four he tells his mum, crying. The school excludes the boy and he pays it all back. An older girl at the bus stop calls Mia «baby» every morning; ignoring her for a month only makes it worse, so Mia tells the bus driver, who makes the girl sit at the front. At that bus stop, Mill Lane kids and the St Mary's lot trade rude names every afternoon, until someone throws a bottle and a boy gets cut; a joke rivalry has turned dangerous. When a big boy snatches her little brother's hat, Lily tells him to give it back, and he does — but she still tells the teacher on duty, in case it happens again. In netball a girl keeps pushing Maddie behind the umpire's back; the one time Maddie shoves back, the umpire sees it and sends Maddie off.
explain: Sometimes the bully is in the family, or even a grown-up. Jake's cousin Dylan, thirteen, holds the remote over his head every Sunday until Jake's auntie tells him to pack it in (stop it). At the cup final, a dad from the other team screams at Connor after a clean tackle; the referee warns the dad, and the coach tells Connor, «That's on him, not you.» And temper can be its own enemy: Jake boots the goalpost after a bad penalty and breaks his big toe. Six weeks on crutches.
fact: A rival is someone you compete against; good rivals shake hands and can push each other to get better.
fact: A bully picks on someone again and again on purpose, often someone smaller or younger.
fact: Water near a weir pulls very hard and is dangerous; never go into a river after a ball.
fact: Bullying by older children is a job for grown-ups; telling a parent or teacher is the right move.
fact: Demanding money with threats is very serious; tell an adult straight away, even if you are scared.
fact: Ignoring a bully sometimes works, but if it gets worse, tell someone in charge.
fact: Being bigger or older does not give anyone the right to bully, even in a family.
fact: Grown-ups can behave badly at sport too; their shouting is not the child's fault.
fact: Hitting back when an umpire isn't looking often gets you caught and lets your team down; tell the coach instead.
fact: Throwing things turns a joke school rivalry into a dangerous one.
fact: Standing up for a younger brother or sister is good, and telling a grown-up as well is better.
fact: Kicking things in anger can break bones; losing hurts less than a broken toe.
exercise: What is the difference between a rival and a bully? | A rival competes with you, a bully picks on you | A rival is always older and bigger than a bully is | There is no difference at all | A bully only plays football
exercise: Mill Lane lost 4–1. What did the captains do after the match? | Shook hands, as the rules say | Refused to look at each other | Started a fight in the car park | Swapped teams for next year
exercise: How did Sam's rival Dev make him better? | Sam trained harder to beat him | Dev taught him to cheat | Dev gave him a pair of special fast goggles | Sam copied Dev's homework
exercise: Big Gaz kicked Sam's ball into the river. What did Sam do? | Went home and told his dad | Jumped in the river to get it | Kicked Gaz's ball in the river too | Followed the ball down to the weir
exercise: Why must you never go into a river after a ball? | The water can pull you away, like at a weir | Balls always come back to the bank on their own | The water is always too warm | Fish don't like footballs
exercise: How did Jake's cousin Dylan's teasing stop? | Jake's auntie told him to pack it in | Jake grew taller than Dylan overnight | Jake hit him with the remote | Dylan moved to Australia
exercise: What did the coach tell Connor after the dad shouted at him? | «That's on him, not you» | «You should cry less» | «You did cheat, mate» | «Shout back next time»
exercise: Ignoring the girl at the bus stop made it worse. What worked for Mia? | Telling the bus driver | Pulling the girl's hair | Walking to school in the rain | Calling her names back louder
exercise: The netball girl kept pushing Maddie. What happened when Maddie shoved back? | The umpire saw it and sent her off | The pushing girl was sent off instead of Maddie | Maddie's team won by ten | Nobody noticed at all
exercise: A Year 6 boy took Kieran's dinner money for days. What made it stop? | Kieran told his mum and she called the school | Kieran gave him even more money so he'd leave him alone | Kieran started bringing no money | The boy got bored after a year
exercise: Why is «give us your money or else» so serious? | It's taking money by threats | It's just borrowing between mates | It's a normal playground game | It's only serious if it's over £10
exercise: What turned the bus stop rivalry between two schools dangerous? | Someone threw a bottle | They shouted rude names | They wore different uniforms | They sang football songs
exercise: Lily told the big boy to give her brother's hat back. What else did she do? | Told the teacher on duty anyway | Stole his hat and hid it in return | Promised never to tell anyone | Hit him with her bag
exercise: Why did Lily still tell a teacher after getting the hat back? | Because it might happen again | Because she wanted him expelled for fun | Because the hat was broken | She didn't, she kept it secret
exercise: Jake booted the goalpost after losing. What happened? | He broke his big toe | The goalpost fell over | The referee changed the score | His team got another penalty
exercise: Does being older give someone the right to bully? | No, size and age give no such right | Yes, if they're in Year 8 or above | Yes, if they're family | Only on Sundays at home
test: «Gaz chucked me ball in the river, it's by the weir, I'm wadin in for it». What's the right answer? | No, stay out and tell a grown-up | Yes, if you're a good swimmer | Yes, quickly before it goes over the weir | Yes, but take your shoes off
test: Kieran: «if I tell, he'll batter me, so I'll just keep paying, it's only a quid». Wise? | No, tell an adult now, threats for money are serious | Yes, paying a quid a day keeps you safe for ever and ever | Yes, a quid doesn't matter | No, bring a bigger kid to fight him
test: Mia's dad says «just ignore her, sweetheart». A month later it's worse. What now? | Tell someone in charge, like the bus driver | Keep ignoring her for another year till she gets bored | Flick the girl's hair back | Stop going to school
test: A dad screams «little cheat!» at a 10-year-old after a fair tackle. Who's in the wrong? | The dad | The 10-year-old | The referee | Both teams
test: «they're well bare annoying, them St Mary's lot». («bare» is London slang for very.) Is shouting fine but throwing things not? | Yes, throwing things can really hurt someone | No, throwing is fine if it's plastic | No, shouting is far worse than throwing | Yes, but only bottles are a problem
test: A girl keeps shoving Maddie behind the umpire's back. What's smartest? | Tell her coach at the break | Shove back hard, she started it | Walk off the court for good | Trip her when nobody's looking
test: «Dylan's just me cousin messin about, family can't be bullies». True? | No, family can bully too, tell a grown-up | Yes, cousins are only joking, even when it hurts | Yes, if they're older it's allowed | No, but you just have to take it
test: What would probably happen if Sam had chased his ball into the river? | He could be pulled under and badly hurt | He'd get the ball back easily | Gaz would say sorry | The weir would stop the ball for him
test: «Dev's my rival so I'll never shake his hand». Better idea? | Shake hands, rivals deserve respect | Never speak to him again | Make him fall in the pool | Quit swimming so you can't ever lose to him
test: Jake: «that penalty was a joke, I'm gonna lamp the post». What's the wiser move? | Walk off the anger without kicking anything | Kick the post as hard as he can to get it all out | Kick the referee instead | Kick the ball at the crowd
test: In a film, the little kid beats up the big bully and everyone claps. What actually works best with an older bully? | Getting a grown-up involved | Fighting him like in the film | Giving him all your stuff | Becoming a bully yourself
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a sporting rival and a bully. | The former competes fairly, whereas the latter persistently intimidates | The former is invariably older than the latter | No distinction exists between the two | The latter is confined to sporting occasions
exam: Ascertain the most prudent course of action for a child whose ball has been propelled into a river flowing towards a weir. | To refrain from entering the water and to inform an adult | To enter the water promptly in order to recover it before the weir | To pursue the ball along the river to the weir | To await its return to the bank
exam: Given that a pupil is compelled under threat to surrender money daily to an older pupil, select the response consistent with the established facts. | The matter is grave and must be reported to an adult forthwith | The payments ought to continue in order to avoid harm | The matter is trivial owing to the small sums involved | The pupil should procure a larger pupil to retaliate
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises verbal abuse directed at a child by an adult spectator at a sporting fixture? | Responsibility lies with the adult and not the child | The child is responsible for having provoked it | Such conduct is customary and acceptable | The referee is obliged to disregard it
